        "IMAGING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "IMAGING",
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Spinal cervical survey radiographs normal.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "MRI—on T2W transverse images of the cervical spine, bilateral and symmetrical well-demarcated, ovoid in shape, intra-axial hyperintensities in the white matter of the dorsolateral funiculi, extending contiguously from the cervicomedullary junction to the sixth cervical vertebral body. Symmetrical hyperintensities also observed in pyramids and ventral aspect of the crus cerebri in the brain. No contrast enhancement.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis normal.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Although the most severe lesion is located in the white matter of the cervical spinal cord, it extends caudally into the thoracic spinal cord and rostrally into the brainstem.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Macroscopic examination—in the dorsal aspect of the lateral funiculi of the cervical spinal cord and in the pyramidal tracts of the medulla oblongata, presence of welldemarcated whitish opaque foci located bilaterally and symmetrically, corresponding to myelin loss.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Microscopy—in the cervical spinal cord, the lesion affects the cervical spinal cord white matter in the dorsal portion of the lateral funiculi (dorsal spinocerebellar, lateral corticospinal, reticulospinal, and rubrospinal tracts). In the brain, the lesion affects the pyramidal tracts, crus cerebri, medial lemniscus, caudal cerebellar peduncle, trapezoid body, spinal tracts of the trigeminal nerve, and optic tracts; multifocal lesions are also observed in the cerebellar folia.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The myelin loss exceeds axon loss with extensive astrogliosis and astrocytosis. Normal-appearing axons observed in areas of severe demyelination.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Lesion bilateral with some asymmetry.",
              "pdf_page": 915,
              "printed_page": 833,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Outpatient, unless the severity of neurologic deficits precludes nursing care at home.",
          "pdf_page": 915,
          "printed_page": 833,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Activity—whatever can be tolerated.",
          "pdf_page": 915,
          "printed_page": 833,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Diet—ensure proper intake of food; patient may have difficulty reaching the feeding area.",
          "pdf_page": 915,
          "printed_page": 833,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Neurologic status—slowly and progressively deteriorates; eventually, the patient is unable to walk or get up.",
          "pdf_page": 915,
          "printed_page": 833,
          "column": 2
        }
      ],
